course: everyday
lesson: 490
title: Comfortable families: enough, but not everything
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Reading, Elsie's mum is a nurse and her dad is a teacher. They're comfortable: enough for the bills, food, the odd treat, but they still count. Elsie wants a £200 gaming headset. «Everyone's parents just buy it!» Dad, a proper Gen X dad, says, «Back in my day we saved up for things. You save half, we'll match it.» So Elsie saves £100, her parents add £100, and when the headset finally arrives it feels twice as good. Comfortable means enough, but not everything.
explain: Most families sit somewhere in the middle, and there's always someone with more and someone with less. In Denver, Ned asks why they haven't got a pool like his friend Maddox. Mom says, «We have what we need and some fun stuff. Maddox's family has more. Lowkey, our camping trips are better» («lowkey» means quietly, between us). In Manchester, Ahmed gets £3 pocket money a week and his friend gets £20. «That's well tight» («well tight» means really stingy). He sulks, then works out what £3 a week can save. Some kids get no pocket money at all.
explain: Even comfortable families have to choose when prices go up. Most families who own their house pay the bank back a bit each month; when Ruby-Mae's family's payment goes up in Sydney, Mum says, «No overseas trip this year, mate.» Ruby-Mae's friends go to Bali. She's gutted (very disappointed), then they go camping and it's heaps fun («heaps» is Aussie for very). In Edinburgh, Torquil loses his £40 scooter at the park and Mum won't replace it. He's raging (Scots for furious), then starts doing chores to earn a new one. Looking after your stuff is part of what money teaches.
explain: In Cardiff, Gran gives Lily-Rose £50 for her birthday and Mum says half goes in savings. «It's MY money!» They agree she'll save £20 and spend £30. In Kent, Ollie-James asks, «Are we rich?» Dad says, «We're lucky. Rich is a big word.» Rich and poor aren't two boxes; families are spread out all along the line, and having enough plus a bit extra is a kind of luck. Remembering it keeps you kind.
explain: Being comfortable means thinking about other people's feelings. In Oxford, Barnaby finds out his friend from the estate gets free school meals and says loudly, «That's lucky, free food!» His friend goes red: free meals mean money is hard at home. Barnaby meant no harm, and the fix is a quick sorry. In Norwich, Frida's mum hands her friend Lacey a bag of Frida's old clothes in front of everyone, and Lacey goes quiet. In Vancouver, Haruka brings a whole bag to the food drive (collecting tins for families who need them) and someone mutters «show-off». The kindest help is quiet and offered, and the other person can always say no.
fact: A comfortable family has enough for bills, food and some treats, but not everything, and still has to make choices.
fact: Most families are somewhere in the middle; there is always someone with more and someone with less.
fact: Saving for something and having a parent match it teaches the value of money, and the thing feels better when you finally get it.
fact: Pocket money is very different from family to family; it isn't a measure of love, and some children get none at all.
fact: Families who own a house usually pay the bank back a bit every month; when that payment goes up, there's less for extras like holidays.
fact: Holidays don't have to be far away to be good; camping or a day out can be just as much fun.
fact: Saving some money and spending some is a good habit.
fact: Rich and poor aren't two boxes; families are spread out between them, and having enough is a kind of luck.
fact: Free school meals mean money is hard at home, so calling them «lucky» can hurt; if you get it wrong, say sorry.
fact: The kindest help is given quietly, not in front of others, and the other person can say no.
fact: Giving more because you have more is good; giving quietly avoids making others feel small.
fact: Losing something has a cost; earning a replacement by chores or saving teaches you to look after things.
fact: Words in this lesson: «lowkey» means quietly or a bit, «well tight» (North England) means really stingy, «gutted» means very disappointed, «heaps» (Australia, NZ) means lots or very, «raging» (Scotland, Ireland) means furious, «back in my day» is what older people say about when they were young.
exercise: What does «comfortable» mean for Elsie's family? | Enough, but not everything | Rich enough for anything at all | Too poor to buy any treats | Comfy chairs in every room
exercise: How much does Elsie have to save for the £200 headset? | £100 | £200 | £50 | £150
exercise: Why does Dad make Elsie save half? | It teaches her what money is worth | He can't afford even one pound of it | He wants her to give up on gaming | He wants to keep the money for himself
exercise: «Back in my day we saved up.» Who usually says «back in my day»? | Older people, about when they were young | Kids, about what they did yesterday | Teachers, about the end of the school day | Shopkeepers, about the day the shop opened
exercise: Ned asks why they haven't got a pool. What's Mom's answer? | They have what they need, Maddox's family has more | Pools are bad for you and Maddox will get ill | They'll build a much bigger pool next summer | They're too poor to ever have any fun stuff
exercise: «Lowkey, our camping trips are better.» What does «lowkey» mean? | Quietly, between us | Very loudly | With the lights off | Not at all
exercise: Ahmed gets £3 and his friend gets £20. What's true? | Pocket money differs, and it isn't a measure of love | Ahmed's parents love him a lot less than his friend's | His friend's parents are the only ones being fair | Everyone in Manchester gets the same pocket money
exercise: «That's well tight.» What does Ahmed mean? | That's really stingy | That's very good | That's too small | That's a bit too loose
exercise: Why can't Ruby-Mae's family go to Bali this year? | The monthly house payment went up | The airport in Sydney has closed | Ruby-Mae was naughty at school | They don't like hot places at all
exercise: How did Ruby-Mae's holiday turn out? | Camping, and it was heaps fun | Bali, with all her friends | Nothing, they stayed indoors | A cruise round the islands
exercise: Barnaby said free meals were «lucky». Why did his friend go red? | Free meals mean money is hard at home | His friend didn't like the dinner that day | Barnaby had stolen his friend's pudding | His friend was too hot in the dinner hall
exercise: What should Barnaby do once he realises? | Say sorry | Say it again louder | Tell the dinner ladies | Ask for free meals too
exercise: Frida's mum gave Lacey old clothes in front of everyone. What would have been kinder? | Offering quietly, so Lacey could say no | Giving her even more clothes the next day | Telling Lacey's mum off for her old clothes | Asking the whole class to bring clothes for her
exercise: How did Lily-Rose's birthday money end up? | £20 saved, £30 spent | £25 saved, £25 spent | £50 spent on sweets | £50 all in savings
exercise: «Are we rich?» What did Ollie-James's dad say? | «We're lucky. Rich is a big word.» | «Yes, we're richer than everyone.» | «No, we're poor, don't ask again.» | «Ask your mother, I've no idea.»
exercise: Someone called Haruka a «show-off» for bringing a whole bag. Was giving more wrong? | No, but giving quietly avoids drama | Yes, everyone must give exactly one can | Yes, giving more is always showing off | No, so she should bring three bags next
exercise: Torquil lost his £40 scooter. What did Mum decide? | He has to save up for a new one | She bought him a new one that day | She bought him two so he has a spare | She banned scooters in the house forever
exercise: «Raging» about the scooter. What did Torquil feel? | Furious | Racing | Rainy | Sleepless
test: «if ur parents don't buy u everything they're poor or mean». Right? | No, comfortable families can't buy everything | Yes, rich parents always buy everything | Yes, saying no means they're mean | No, it means they're secretly rich
test: Elsie saves £5 a week for her £100 half. How many weeks does it take? | 20 | 10 | 40 | 5
test: Ahmed saves all of his £3 a week. After how many weeks can he afford a £15 football? | 5 | 3 | 15 | 12
test: Lily-Rose: «Mum wanted half in savings and I only saved £20». How much less than half did she save? | £5 | £20 | £25 | £10
test: «Maddox has a pool so his family's better than ours». What's true? | Having more money doesn't make a family better | Families with pools are better than others | Ned's family must be poor without a pool | Maddox's family is bad for having a pool
test: In a cartoon, the middle-class kid throws a tantrum and gets a pony the same day. What's more real? | Families make choices, and tantrums don't buy ponies | Every tantrum ends with a pony if it's loud enough | Middle-class families always have stables at home | Ponies are cheaper than headsets, so they say yes
test: What would most likely happen if Torquil's mum bought a new scooter every time he lost one? | He wouldn't learn to look after his stuff | He would never lose a scooter again | The park would give the scooters back | His mum would save loads of money
test: Barnaby: «free food, lucky you!» His friend goes red and quiet. «Wot, it's true innit». What's the better next move? | «Sorry, that came out wrong» | «Chill, I'm only joking, mate» | «Can I have your pudding then?» | «Why are you all red, weirdo?»
test: Ollie-James's mate says, «you're minted, you've got two cars». («Minted» means very rich.) Which fits what his dad said? | They're lucky to have enough, but «rich» is a big word | Two cars means they're the richest family in Kent | Cars are free, so it doesn't mean anything at all | They must be poor if they need two cars to get about
test: Haruka wants to keep giving without anyone calling her a show-off. What could she do next time? | Drop the bag in quietly | Give nothing at all | Bring a sign with her name | Tell everyone how much it cost
test: Ruby-Mae: «Bali or nothing, camping's sooo mid». («Mid» means boring, average.) What did she find out? | Camping turned out heaps fun | Camping really was boring and awful | Her friends were all in Bali sulking | Bali was cancelled because of rain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a «comfortable» family? | One possessing sufficient means, though not unlimited | One possessing unlimited means for every purchase | One possessing insufficient means for necessities | One possessing furniture of exceptional comfort
exam: Given that a parent undertakes to match a child's savings towards an item costing two hundred pounds, ascertain the sum the child must save. | One hundred pounds | Two hundred pounds | Fifty pounds | Four hundred pounds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning differences in pocket money between families. | They are considerable and are no measure of parental affection | They are prescribed by law and identical for children of an age | They reflect precisely the love each parent bears the child | They are determined by the school and paid by the head teacher
exam: Identify the most considerate manner of offering assistance to a friend of lesser means. | Quietly, permitting the friend to decline | Publicly, so that all may observe it | Repeatedly, whether or not it is wanted | Through the friend's teacher, before the class
